# Line endings and binary handling, set deliberately rather than left to defaults.
#
# Why this file exists at all: every text file in this tree is LF (measured — zero CR lines in
# docs/SPEC.md, scripts/*.sh, manifests/* and .gitignore). Git for Windows defaults core.autocrlf
# to true, which would convert the whole tree on the next checkout. A shell script with CRLF line
# endings does not fail in a way that looks like a line-ending problem under WSL: `#!/bin/bash\r`
# is not the interpreter anyone asked for, and `set -euo pipefail\r` fails in the middle of a
# multi-hour build. So the conversion has to be refused in the tree, not remembered by each clone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Named explicitly because they are executed by a Linux shell inside WSL, and a single CR in any
# of them costs a run that is already ~100 minutes deep.
*.sh          text eol=lf
live/auto/config text eol=lf

# Manifests are read by scripts/gen-package-lists.sh, which treats each non-comment line as a
# package name. A trailing \r becomes part of the name and apt fails on it.
manifests/*.pkgs text eol=lf

# Cited evidence is never rewritten. §11 makes these logs the proof behind claims in SPEC.md, and
# logs/evidence/{M0-evidence,M1-boot-artifacts}-sha256.txt are checksum manifests over files that
# are themselves in this tree — normalise a single byte of any of them and a committed manifest
# starts lying about committed bytes. Measured: three cited logs already contain a CR line
# (logs/M1-explicit-attribution.txt, logs/M1-shipped-dpkg.txt), which is exactly the content that
# a text=auto pass would change.
logs/**   -text
docs/SPEC.md text eol=lf

# Screenshots and signed material are bytes, not text.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.gz        binary
*.xz        binary
*.deb       binary
*.iso       binary
*.key.chroot binary
*.gpg       binary
*.asc       binary
